WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.002
-Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-winning actor.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.704
He narrates
"Life on Our Planet,"

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.139
which is streaming
now on Netflix.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:08.541
Please welcome back to the show
Morgan Freeman, everybody.

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:10.543
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:24.891 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:26.959
Welcome back!
-Thank you.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.427
Nice to be back.
-It's nice to have you --

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:30.430
It's nice to have you here,
back in New York City.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.531
-Thank you.
-When did you first

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:34.467
come to the city?

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:41.574
-I first came in
nineteen sixty...one, I think.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.643
-All right.
-'60, '60.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:46.345
1959 -- 1960.
-1960.

00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:48.781
-But I only stayed five months.
-Okay.

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:51.484
-And then I fled.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.386
And I went to San Francisco.
-Okay.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.488
-Stayed in San Francisco
for about three years

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.724
and then came back more ready.

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.892
-Oh, okay.
So did you -- as an actor

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:02.761
more ready or --
-Yep. As a performer.

00:01:02.761 --> 00:01:04.630
-Okay. Gotcha.
-'Cause I had taken dance.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.232
-Oh, that's right.
You did it all.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:08.000
Yeah, you did it all.
I remember we talked --

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.702
You danced at the World's Fair
here in New York City.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.304
-Yeah, I --
[ Chuckles ]

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:13.406
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.473
You know that?

00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:15.908
-I mean, you're the only person

00:01:15.908 --> 00:01:18.111
I've ever met who's danced
at a World's Fair.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:22.348
-Right. 1984 World's Fair,
New York world's fair.

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.350
-That's amazing.
So, you come back in 1963,

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.684
you're a performer.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.684 --> 00:01:28.854
-And how did you --
how did you find New York City?

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:31.690
-In 1963?
-Yeah.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.693
-Well, it's probably the most
exciting place, you know,

00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:40.766
particularly that I was ready to
make the move into the big-time.

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:42.435
By that, I mean get a job.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.737
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:46.572
-Just a paying job?
-Right.

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:49.943
-And now do you was -- your
first New York City apartment --

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.644
Probably not a penthouse
with a beautiful view

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.746
of the park or anything.
-No, no, no.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:59.686
I had one-room
in a place on 12th Street

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:04.390
and Avenue A,
called Paradise Alley.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.327
-All right. And was it -- was it
more Paradise or more Alley?

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.961
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:11.631
-It was enough of both
to get the name.

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.699
-There you go.

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:19.705
-A lot of young dancers,
singers, musicians,

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:22.741
actors lived there.
And it wasn't --

00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:26.712
It wasn't subsidized.
It was just affordable.

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:29.115
-You were doing --
How long in New York

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.017
before you did "Hello, Dolly!"
on Broadway?

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:34.420
-I did "Hello, Dolly!"
on Broadway.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.623
I got the job in November '67.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:40.259
-And I worked there
for 11 months.

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:41.727
-And was that
one of the first,

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.329
"I think I've made it"
moments for you?

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.464
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.131
-And was doing a show
for 11 months,

00:02:46.131 --> 00:02:48.201
was it exciting the whole time?

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:50.069
-I was working
with Pearl Bailey.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.572
-There you go.
-Every night was a joy.

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:54.173
-That's pretty exciting.
-Yeah.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:56.476
-You've also -- you also
had shows that had

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.478
shorter runs on Broadway.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:02.147
-What was your show
that only had nine performances?

00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:03.916
-"The Mighty Jets."
-"The Mighty Jets."

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.351
-Yeah.
-Did you have any sense

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.821
on the first night that it would
only be there for nine nights?

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.791
-I had that sense
while we were rehearsing.

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:15.828
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.263
That's the truth.
-Yeah.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:19.532
So kind of sometimes you know?
-Yeah, sometimes, you know,

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:21.667
"This is not going to work."

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:24.036
-Yeah. Now, an interesting
thing happened, though,

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.038
because the show
only had nine performances,

00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:28.174
but you received
a Tony nomination.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.743
-Yes.
-Is that sort of cold comfort

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.412
when you receive praise

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:34.314
for a show
that had such a short run?

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:36.482
-It is. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:38.551
-Operative word,
however, is comfort.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.820
-Uh-huh.
-Yeah. I mean, it's like,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.821
"Okay."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.821 --> 00:03:44.323
-Who decided --

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:47.126
Was it something you decided,
"I'm going to be a performer,"

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.894
or was it decided for you?

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:50.562
-It was decided for me.
-Yeah? How about?

00:03:50.562 --> 00:03:52.531
-I was born.
-How long? How old were you?

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:54.167
-I was born.
-You were born, yeah.

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:57.169
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:58.437
-Yeah.
-That's a fact.

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.139
-Yeah.
-Sorry.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.442
-Did you know -- at what age
did you think,

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:04.811
"Oh, I'm gonna be better
at this than other things"?

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:09.114
-I don't know.
-Yeah.

00:04:09.114 --> 00:04:14.520
-I just was always able.
I always went whole hog.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.123
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:21.793
-When we played --
Like, I had a friend

00:04:21.793 --> 00:04:25.364
when I was like 5, 6.

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:27.500
And we played, like,
horse and buggy.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.970
You just put a clothesline,
a rope, around your waist,

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.107
and he holds on to it
and goes -- [ Clicks tongue ]

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:37.643
[ Laughter ]

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:39.778
-It's still -- sometimes kids
who have the talent

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:44.850
don't have the confidence
to then go do a school play

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.452
or join the theater club.
Was that something --

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.487
As soon as you got to school --
-I had a mother

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:51.124
who was very into --
-Oh, that helps.

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:55.995
-Yeah.
And I had three brothers

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:59.198
who -- and we were all,
on some level,

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:02.001
artistic, you know?

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:03.503
-We're gonna take
a quick break

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:06.472
and then talk about this Netflix
documentary you're narrating.

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:08.073
But I wonder,
you get asked so often,

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:11.177
what's the worst thing
you've been asked to narrate?

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:16.148
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.618
-I mean, you know,
somebody's telephone number.

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:20.185
-Oh, people ask you
to leave messages and stuff?

00:05:20.185 --> 00:05:21.320
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.055
I don't --
You don't seem to me

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:26.225
like the kind of guy
who would like doing that.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:29.962
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.798
-Don't -- Don't -- Don't --
Don't get me into trouble.

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:33.399
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:35.735
-We'll be right back with more
from Morgan Freeman.

